:"Combined Fire Authority for County Durham and Darlington Pension Board Committee","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-16","committee":"Adults, Wellbeing and Health Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Special","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Environment and Sustainable Communities Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"1.30 pm","status":"Confirmed; Special","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Local Pension Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Economy and Enterprise Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Adults, Wellbeing and Health Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Special","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"County Durham faces \u00a383m four-year budget shortfall threatening local control","lead":"County Durham faces a four-year budget deficit of \u00a383.7 million over 2027/28 to 2030/31, \u00a338.9 million worse than forecast last year. Planned savings of \u00a321.5 million leave a \u00a362.1 million gap, requiring either higher council tax or service cuts. The immediate crisis lies in 2027/28 and 2028/29, when the cumulative cash shortfall of \u00a349.7 million will exceed the council's reserves (money set aside) of \u00a334.6 million. The council warns that such over-reliance on reserves has forced other councils into Exceptional Financial Support, a government regime that removes local control of spending\u201436 councils faced this in 2026/27, up from 29 the year before. Adult social care costs heighten the pressure: \u00a334.4 million in fee uplifts over four years against only \u00a325.9 million raised by the dedicated precept, the extra council tax for this service.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Cabinet 16.09.26 - 2026-27 Corporate Performance Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s228665/228665.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"County Durham faces \u00a383.7m budget shortfall over four years as care costs and demand surge","lead":"County Durham's Cabinet heard on 16 September that the council faces a four-year budget deficit of \u00a383.7 million from 2027/28, a deterioration of \u00a338.9 million since last year's financial forecast. The gap has widened as demand for children in care and adult social care has grown more complex and expensive. Rising National Living Wage costs are driving \u00a334.4 million in adult social care fee uplifts across four years, but the dedicated social care precept \u2014 a council tax surcharge \u2014 will raise only \u00a325.9 million, leaving an \u00a38.5 million gap. Children in care numbers have fallen slightly to 1,286, but the council notes demand is shifting toward higher-cost placements for older children with trauma. The council has identified \u00a321.5 million of savings so far, leaving \u00a362.1 million still to find. A two-year cash shortfall of \u00a349.7 million exceeds the council's available reserves of \u00a334.6 million, raising the risk that without deeper cuts or larger council tax rises, the authority may need to seek Exceptional Financial Support \u2014 government intervention that removes local control.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Cabinet 16.09.26 - 2026-27 Corporate Performance Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s228665/228665.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"County Durham faces \u00a384m four-year budget deficit with reserves risk, warns Cabinet","lead":"County Durham's Cabinet heard on 16 September that the council faces a forecasted budget deficit of \u00a383.7 million over the next four years \u2014 a position \u00a338.9 million worse than predicted just a year ago. The council has identified \u00a321.5 million of savings but that leaves a shortfall of \u00a362.1 million to close. More immediately, the cumulative cash deficit in 2027/28 and 2028/29 alone will reach \u00a349.7 million, exceeding the council's unallocated reserves of \u00a334.6 million. The council warned that authorities forced to over-rely on reserves have been compelled into Exceptional Financial Support, a government intervention removing local control; 36 councils entered this regime in 2026/27, up from 29 the previous year. Closing the four-year gap will require either significant council tax rises or savings deeper than currently planned.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Cabinet 16.09.26 - 2026-27 Corporate Performance Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s228665/228665.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"05 -Audit Committee 30.09.26 - Compliance with IAS 2026 Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s229458/229458.pdf","headline":"Council detected and prevented approximately \u00a31.133 million in fraud, overpayments and error during 2025/26.","detail":"Corporate Fraud Team received 1,117 referrals and retained 709 cases for investigation. Fraud identified/prevented comprised \u00a3505,380 from direct corporate fraud and \u00a3627,590 from housing fraud via partner arrangements. Highest-volume areas were Council Tax Fraud (513 referrals) and Council Tax Reduction Scheme fraud (332 referrals). Team delivered 38 prosecutions and sanctions. Management confirms no material impact on statement of accounts.","category":"audit","matters":"Demonstrates active and effective fraud detection capability protecting council assets. High referral volume reflects high-fraud-risk environment (benefits, discounts, taxes) but effective controls with significant recoveries indicate strong financial safeguards.","figures":"\u00a31.133m identified/prevented (\u00a3505,380 corporate; \u00a3627,590 housing); 1,117 referrals; 709 cases retained (63%); 38 prosecutions and sanctions","quote":"The Team identified or prevented fraud, overpayments and error valued at approximately \u00a31.133 million, comprising \u00a3505,380 from direct corporate fraud investigations and a further \u00a3627,590 from housing-related fraud investigations undertaken through partnership arrangements with Registered Social Landlords.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"05 -Audit Committee 30.09.26 - Compliance with IAS 2026 Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s229458/229458.pdf","headline":"External LGA peer review found council is financially stable and well-managed with reserves at accepted sector levels.","detail":"October 2025 Local Government Association peer review concluded Durham County Council is 'financially stable organisation with strong, unified culture' delivering 'high-performing services and outcomes relative to the rest of the sector.' Financial management section found council 'financially well-managed' with 'levels of debt and reserves consistent with accepted sector practice, and it consistently produces timely accounts.'","category":"governance","matters":"Independent external validation by LGA provides credible assessment of council's financial management quality relative to sector peers, relevant to lenders evaluating creditworthiness and financial stability.","figures":"None","quote":"Durham County Council is a financially well-managed and stable organisation. Its levels of debt and reserves are consistent with accepted sector practice, and it consistently produces timely accounts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"05 -Audit Committee 30.09.26 - Compliance with IAS 2026 Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s229458/229458.pdf","headline":"No events identified that would cast doubt on the council's ability to continue as a going concern.","detail":"Audit Committee confirmed on 31 July 2026 that going concern basis is appropriate for the financial statements. No events or conditions identified since then that would create doubt about the council's ability to operate and discharge liabilities over the next 12+ months.","category":"governance","matters":"Going concern status is fundamental to lenders' assessment of a council's financial viability and ability to service debt over the medium term.","figures":"Assessment period to 31 March 2026; forward-looking period of at least 12 months","quote":"It did not identify any event that would cast a significant doubt on the organisation's ability to continue as a going concern.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"06 Annual Report of the Audit Committee for the period September 25 to August 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s229399/229399.pdf","headline":"Special Audit Committee meeting on 26 March 2026 examined the Toft Hill and High Etherley Bypass scheme's governance, decision-making and value for money, with focus on lessons learned.","detail":"A dedicated audit committee meeting was convened to review this capital project, specifically examining governance and value-for-money aspects, with focus on understanding what was known at the time, how decisions were made, and what lessons could be taken forward. The fact that this project warranted a special audit review suggests governance or value-for-money considerations merited specific scrutiny.","category":"capital","matters":"Special audit scrutiny of a capital project indicates it merited close examination of governance and value-for-money execution, which would concern a lender assessing the council's project delivery and capital management.","figures":"Not specified in report.","quote":"The Committee received a presentation of the Corporate Director of Neighbourhoods and Environment which presented the details of the development of the Toft Hill and High Etherley Bypass scheme considering the governance, decision-making and value of money in relation to the project, with a focus on understanding what was known at the time, how decisions were made, and what lessons could be taken forward.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"06 Annual Report of the Audit Committee for the period September 25 to August 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s229399/229399.pdf","headline":"Children in Care cost pressures are driving increasing financial pressures that the Council is actively managing and seeking to mitigate.","detail":"The Audit Committee specifically requested a presentation on 27 February 2026 from the Corporate Director of Children and Young People's Services to understand factors driving increasing cost pressures in Children in Care. The presentation covered the use of unregulated and crisis placements and the arrangements and controls in place over such placements, indicating this is an active area of financial and operational risk management.","category":"overspend","matters":"Material cost pressures in children's services are a key financial sustainability issue for councils and directly relevant to a lender's assessment of financial position and budget management capability.","figures":"Not quantified in the report.","quote":"A presentation by the Corporate Director of Children and Young People's Services which provided an overview of the factors driving the increasing cost pressures associated with Children in Care and the action the Council was taking to manage and mitigate those cost pressures, as requested by the Audit Committee.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Durham UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":true,"headline":"Long-term borrowing costs now at 6%+, exceeding council budget assumptions of 5-5.5%, creating headwind as capital programme normalises","lead":"Rising cost of debt ahead. Under-borrowed position is a timing strategy, not a solution to rate pressures. PWLB rates at 6%+ will materially increase future financing costs as capital spends progress. Limited ability to absorb further capital pressures\u2026","also":["24 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Children and Young People's Overview and Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-25","n_findings":25,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Cabinet 16.09.26 - QTR1 Forecast of Outturn 2026-27 (003)","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.durham.gov.uk/documents/s228673/228673.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":102,"news_drafts_total":3}}